  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The formula broke when it mattered most.",
    "what_this_means": "Arizona came for four runs in the third and didn't look back, and by the time Bryce Eldridge found two answers in the fourth, the Giants were already chasing a deficit they could not close. They had the moments. Runners in scoring position six times. But converted just twice, left eight on the bases, and never threatened after the sixth. Blade Tidwell gave them five quality innings; Spencer Bivens gave them back four runs in two. The bullpen held after that, but the game was gone by then. This is what inconsistency looks like in real time: enough good pieces to stay in it, never enough finish to win it.",
    "what_to_watch": "They scored when they had runners on base. That part worked. But the game was decided by who didn't show up: the clutch swing in the fifth, the one clean inning after Arizona pulled ahead to stay. The lineup struck out eleven times. Against a Diamondbacks team that's beatable, that's not a margin. That's a surrender. Eldridge carried what he could, but one bat never carries a game to victory. What cost them was not the batting order or the bullpen or the starter. It was the gap between having chances and finishing them. Down four runs late is not a comeback waiting to happen. It's a loss already written. Eight left on base. Zero answer.",
